theory CommonModel

begin

// Shared assumptions for OAuth2 models.
// Channels, agents, and attacker capabilities.
// Note: This theory is verified standalone and NOT included from others
// due to CI constraints. Protocol-specific files redeclare the facts they use.

rule InitConfidentialClient:
  [ ] --[ Confidential('clientA') ]-> [ ]

rule ValidBasicAuth:
  [ ] --[ ValidAuth($C, 'basic') ]-> [ ]

rule ValidPostAuth:
  [ ] --[ ValidAuth($C, 'post') ]-> [ ]

rule ValidPrivateKeyJwt:
  [ ] --[ ValidAuth($C, 'pkjwt') ]-> [ ]

// Public network: modeled by Tamarin's built-in Dolev-Yao adversary.
// - Any term in an Out(M) fact is learned by the attacker.
// - The attacker can provide any term it can derive via In(M).

// Attacker capabilities (Dolev-Yao):
// - Attacker can observe and forward messages on the public network via In/Out.
// - No rule connects SSend/SRecv with In/Out, modeling a secure channel the
//   attacker cannot observe or forge.

// Registration and policy placeholders
rule RegisterRedirectUri:
  [ ] --[ Registered($C, $RU) ]-> [ ]

rule AllowScope:
  [ ] --[ AllowedScope($C, $S) ]-> [ ]

rule AllowGrant:
  [ ] --[ AllowedGrant($C, $G) ]-> [ ]

// HTTPS-like protected channel abstraction (no attacker observation here)
rule SecureDeliver:
  [ ] --[ SSend($A, $B, $M) ]-> [ SRecv($A, $B, $M) ]

// Note: No rule from SSend/SRecv to In/Out is provided, modeling a
// protected channel between endpoints A and B that the attacker
// cannot intercept or forge on.

// Freshness helpers used by other models (redeclared there when needed)
// Fr(x) facts are used to denote nonces, codes, jti, etc. as freshly generated.

end
